.TH WINFO 9
.SH NAME
winfo \- Ask about a widget
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f5winfo class \fIwindow\fR
.br
\f5winfo children \fIwindow\fR
.br
\f5winfo manager \fIwindow\fR
.br
\f5winfo master \fIwindow\fR

.SH DESCRIPTION
Each form returns one fact about
.IR window ,
which must exist.
.TP
.B class
The widget's type, as
.B frame
or
.B label
or
.BR button .
.TP
.B children
The paths one level below
.IR window ,
separated by spaces, in the order the widgets were created, and empty if
there are none.
A child is a widget whose path is
.I window
followed by a dot and one further name;
.B .f
has the child
.B .f.b
but not
.BR .f.b.c .
This is by name, so a widget that exists but has never been laid out is
still a child.
.TP
.B manager
The geometry manager that has this widget \(em
.B pack
or
.B grid
\(em or empty if none has it.
A widget can be created and never laid out;
.B appl/wm/toolbar.b
makes a button solely to ask how tall a button would be, and never packs
it.
Such a widget still reports a rectangle, its own border, so this is what
separates one that is on the page from one that is not.
.TP
.B master
The path of the widget this one is laid out inside, or empty if none.
This is not always the parent by name:
.B "pack .b -in .f"
leaves
.B .b
named under the root and lays it out inside
.BR .f ,
so anything reasoning about which widgets share space wants this rather
than the path.
.SH EXAMPLE
Walk a whole window, printing what each widget is and where it sits:
.PP
.EX
	winfo children .
	winfo class .f.ok
	winfo master .f.ok
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR tklayout (2),
.IR pack (9),
.IR grid (9)
.SH BUGS
Real Tk's
.B winfo
answers some forty questions; this one answers four.
.PP
There is no
.BR ismapped .
.B Manager
is the nearest thing, and differs: a widget inside an unmapped parent
still reports its manager.
